    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup is headlined by **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, who boasts the day\u2019s highest efficiency score at 85.99. Facing Kohl Drake (xERA 6.26), **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** is in an elite spot to produce, bolstered by a significant +19% SP skill advantage and a prime #2 lineup position. Not far behind, **Bryce Harper** (80.21) is a must-start; he benefits from a highly favorable +16% park factor and tailwinds that could push a strong performance against Shane Baz. In the outfield, **Tyler Freeman** (72.83) rounds out our top-tier tier, leveraging a matchup against the soft-tossing Luinder Avila to maximize run production, while **Luis Arraez** (65.56) remains a high-floor play at #1, where his +10% order bonus and strong platoon edge against Walker Buehler keep him locked into the top of our daily hierarchy.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision involved keeping **Jonathan Aranda** on the bench despite his strong 62.54 score. While **Jonathan Aranda**\u2019s profile is compelling against the handedness of Hicks, he narrowly lost out to **Kyle Karros** (63.36), whose inclusion was driven by the specific synergy of a +14% park boost and the favorable matchup against Luinder Avila. Meanwhile, we are managing several risks; **Yainer Diaz** (40.31) is barely clinging to a start against the formidable Jacob deGrom, with the score heavily suppressed by a -19% SP skill penalty. We also continue to monitor the \"Lineup Pending\" status for most of our starters; should late-breaking news displace **Cal Raleigh** or **Geraldo Perdomo** from their projected spots, their efficiency scores will likely see downward adjustments. We have kept **Dylan Beavers** and **Daulton Varsho** on the bench as secondary options, but their respective matchups against difficult lefties like Cristopher S\u00e1nchez and Mathews made them statistically inferior to our current starting outfield and utility configuration.",
